  [3mAssumption I'm making (say if it's wrong):[0m the settings module is [1mbrowser-only[0m. [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m and [38;5;153msrc/utils.js[39m are
  CommonJS and a disconnected [38;5;153mgreet[39m demo — an ESM settings file can't be [38;5;153mrequire[39md from them anyway, and wiring both
  worlds together is a much bigger change than you asked for. So [38;5;153msettings.js[39m lives next to [38;5;153mapp.js[39m, not in [38;5;153msrc/[39m.

  [1mThe fork:[0m does [38;5;153mapp.js[39m read a full endpoint URL, or a base URL it builds paths from?

  - [1mBase URL[0m ([38;5;153mapiBaseUrl: "https://api.example.com"[39m, and [38;5;153mlogin()[39m composes [38;5;153m${apiBaseUrl}/login[39m): one entry per
    environment no matter how many endpoints you add later. Costs: path-joining logic lives at the call sites, and you
    need a rule about trailing slashes.
  - [1mFull endpoint URLs[0m ([38;5;153mloginUrl: "https://api.example.com/login"[39m): call sites stay dead simple — exactly today's shape,
    just relocated. Costs: every new endpoint multiplies by the number of environments, and the same host gets
    repeated.

  Cheap to change later either way while there's one endpoint — this gets expensive only once several call sites exist,
  which is exactly why I'd rather pick deliberately now.

  [1mMy recommendation: base URL.[0m "Easier to change environments" is precisely the case where you want [3mone[0m thing per
  environment to edit, and the second endpoint you add will prove it out.
